## The counter-case, honestly presented

The strongest opposing case runs like this.

American knows its own 2043 network best. It has put its premium capital in Terminals C and D and a Provisions grab-and-go in Terminal F. The airport is being asked to override that signal by preloading premium bones the tenant has not requested and will not fund.[^4] Doing so inverts the industry allocation of premium risk, which puts premium fit-out on the carrier's balance sheet — Delta's ~40,000-sf Delta One JFK T4 lounge is carrier-funded fit-out on Port Authority base building.[^42]

American is simultaneously *expanding* the connecting bank from 9 to 13 — the opposite physical implication of a premium-shift brief.[^19] Delta, the acknowledged premium leader, has delayed its own Delta One Lounge at ATL to 2028 because it has not yet worked out how to execute the concept at a major connecting hub.[^43] Designing $4B of DFW concrete around a premium anchor for the industry's follower is betting on an answer even the leader has not published.

American's premium history includes hard retrenchment: in March 2020 it closed every Flagship Lounge except JFK — DFW, LAX, MIA, ORD — for roughly 18 months.[^44] The balance sheet still constrains the pivot: Q1 2026 total debt of $34.7B (below $35B for the first time since 2015, but roughly double United's debt per revenue dollar), a 0.2% net margin in 2025, and a decade-old $3B/$5B profit gap to United and Delta that CEO Robert Isom has framed the premium program as the vehicle to close.[^45] Underwriting premium-forward capital on the assumption that the follower closes that gap by 2035 is an execution risk American itself has not yet reduced.

Modular's ~30% saving is a repetition dividend, not an infrastructure dividend: every bespoke MEP tree for a future Flagship kitchen, every off-standard module, costs the factory its repeat-part cadence. And the bankruptcy record cuts both ways. AMR's 2011 Chapter 11 saw 554 non-residential property leases assumed against 12 rejected in a single September 2013 order — a 2% rejection rate.[^46] American does not reach for §365 as a routine lever — though the record does not show how much of the "assumed" outcome was priced into renegotiations at the threshold of rejection. The threat has value inside a 17-year window.

Every preserved option carries a premium, and options accumulate into shopping lists no future budget funds. The narrow concession that would make the thesis defensible: preserve utility risers and structural allowance on a small, named number of gate positions, on a fully signed American cost-recovery memorandum, and refuse everything else.

## Why the counter-case is insufficient — and where it isn't

The counter-case is right about three things, and the recommendation concedes them: Provisions is a live signal; the industry norm puts premium fit-out on the carrier's balance sheet, so DFW should not preload the *lounge*; and every preserved option carries a premium, so option-pricing discipline is the register's operating rule. Hence the signed American cost-recovery memorandum above $10M, and hence no recommendation that DFW *build* a Flagship Lounge — only that it preserve the envelope American can later fill, on its own decision and balance sheet, at fit-out cost rather than retrofit cost.

The counter-case fails on two counts.

**First, the historical record.** The buildings that absorbed change without material retrofit — Detroit, LaGuardia, T5 — each encoded hub *form*: MARS-capable minority stands, generic connecting-bank geometry, an FIS sized to the international peak of its era, redundant vertical circulation at the transit node. Those are geometry bets on the class of hub Terminal F is, not premium bets on American's 2035 strategy. The counter-case's discipline about carrier-specific premium spend is right; extending it to refuse generic hub geometry is precisely the error the record identifies.

**Second, the asymmetry between preserving an option and retrofitting it.** Concourse D at Dulles omitted an AeroTrain station — cheap at pour, generational to reverse: the mobile-lounge dependency it preserved has run since 1962, and the correction is now budgeted at ~$3.75B inside a $22.5B MWAA program running 2028–2033.[^16] Denver spent more than $100M on terminal-design changes for a baggage system United abandoned in 2005.[^47] The Category A options — low-single-digit percent of a $4B program — are orders of magnitude cheaper than the retrofits they hedge, even discounted for the probability an option is never triggered.

The counter-case's narrow position — buy a few named options with signed AA cost-recovery, refuse everything else — is not the register's opponent. It is the register's operating rule at the *lounge* item. It is not the rule at the *geometry* items, because MARS, FIS envelope, and Skylink redundancy are generic hub bets, and the tenant does not need to sign a memorandum for the airport to buy the shape of its own building. Right in the narrow; insufficient in the general.

### What 2035 has to look like for the recommendation to be wrong

**Three falsifiers.** The recommendation is wrong if by 2035 American has funded a Flagship-tier lounge at Terminal F ahead of what the register anticipated — the shell was unnecessarily conservative, at a cost of a few years' foregone lease revenue inside the $32–49M negative carry. It is wrong if connecting share has fallen materially below the 55% floor — plausibly A321XLR-scale narrowbody flying eroding the connect premium — leaving connecting-hall depth, gate-hold sizing, and Skylink capacity overbuilt.[^48] And it is wrong if CPE has breached the ~$20–22 behavioral threshold [VERIFY: airline-commercial professional judgment, not a sourced figure] — plausible given DWU's FY26 projection at $16.99 and debt projected at $12.4B by FY29 — and American is contesting every optional dollar.[^49] That third case is the largest failure mode — and note the implication: a CPE breach *strengthens* the case for buying options at pour prices, because mid-lease retrofit debt inside a hostile CPE environment is the scenario the airport most wants to avoid.

**Three leading indicators inside 24 months**, each carried on its card: an American side-letter refusal on the Flagship shell allowance (Card 3 converts to airport-side sunk conduit at ~40% of full-shell cost); a CBP letter capping arrivals staffing below the 2043 forecast peak (Card 4 converts to envelope-sized-to-CBP-cap, RSP funding the delta); an FY27 CPE trajectory breaching ~$18, which freezes new Category A adoption until Board reauthorization.

